GETCWD(3)                      P32W Unix                       GETCWD(3)

NAME
       getcwd - get the name of the current directory

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       char *getcwd(char *buf, size_t size);

DESCRIPTION
       getcwd puts the full path name of the current directory, a
       NUL-ended string, into buf, which has room for size bytes.

       The kernel does not keep the name; getcwd finds it as pwd
       did on V7, by climbing: it stats ., then looks in .. for the
       entry with the same inode number, then in ../.. for that of
       .., and so on up to /. Where a directory is the root of a
       mounted filesystem it looks for the entry whose stat(2)
       matches instead.

       With buf NULL, getcwd allocates the buffer with malloc(3),
       at least size bytes and big enough for the name; the caller
       frees it.

RETURN VALUE
       buf (or the new buffer), or NULL with errno set.

ERRORS
       ERANGE     the name, NUL included, is longer than size,
                  or than 255 characters (or the directory is
                  more than 84 levels below /)
       EACCES     a directory on the way up cannot be read or
                  searched
       and the other errors of stat(2) and opendir(3). If a
       directory is not found in its parent (it was removed or
       moved meanwhile), getcwd returns NULL.

EXAMPLES
       char dir[PATH_MAX];
       if (getcwd(dir, sizeof dir) == NULL)
           perror("getcwd");

SEE ALSO
       pwd(1), chdir(2), stat(2), dirent(3)

BUGS
       Paths longer than 255 characters cannot be named. It is
       slow, reading every directory on the way up.
